Your AI coding tool can work directly against platformOS: validate Liquid, GraphQL and YAML **before** a file is written, deploy, read the instance error log, run GraphQL queries and Liquid snippets on a live instance, and run your tests.

All of it together is the **platformOS Agent Kit**: a setup command, four MCP servers, and a playbook of rules and knowledge that runs inside your editor. One command sets it up.

{% include 'alert/note', content: 'This page assumes you have already [installed pos-cli](/get-started/installation-and-configuration/#install-pos-cli) and [authenticated an environment](/get-started/working-with-the-code-and-files/#authenticate-your-environment). Every tool that talks to an instance reads its credentials from the `.pos` file in your project, so set that up first.' %}

## What you get

`pos-cli ai init` sets up two kinds of thing: four MCP servers, and — for Claude Code — the Agent Playbook. [MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/) is the protocol AI tools use to call external tools; your tool starts the servers itself and you never run them by hand.

| Piece | Runs | What it gives the agent |
|---|---|---|
| `platformos-cli` | on your machine | Operations on your instance: deploy and preview a deploy, fetch the error log, run GraphQL and Liquid, fetch a page, run tests, lint the project |
| `platformos-supervisor` | on your machine | One tool, `validate_code`: checks Liquid, GraphQL and YAML **before** the agent writes a file |
| `platformos-liquid` | hosted by platformOS | The Liquid reference — every tag, filter and object, looked up by name |
| `platformos-graphql` | hosted by platformOS | The GraphQL reference — operations, types, arguments and nullability |
| platformOS Agent Playbook | in your project | Rules, topic files and a platformOS coding agent. Not a server — files your editor reads |

Only `platformos-cli` uses your credentials and acts on your instance. `platformos-supervisor` runs locally and reads only the project directory it was started in — it checks code and never contacts an instance. The two hosted servers take no credentials and run no code either; they only answer questions about the published Liquid and GraphQL reference, which is why registering them for all your projects is a reasonable thing to do.

The difference this makes in practice: platformOS Liquid is its own dialect — it has tags and filters other Liquid implementations do not, and lacks some they do — and platformOS has GraphQL shapes of its own. The usual failure is not an error message — it is a page that deploys, returns `200`, and does the wrong thing. An agent that can look a symbol up and validate a buffer before writing it does not have to guess.

## Set up your AI tool

Run this in your project root:

<pre class="command-line" data-user="user" data-host="host"><code class="language-bash">
pos-cli ai init
</code></pre>

It asks up to three questions:

1. **Which AI tool do you use?** — Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code, opencode, or Other. "Other" prints a JSON snippet to paste into any MCP-capable tool yourself.
2. **Also register the documentation servers?** — the hosted Liquid and GraphQL reference, for this project or for all your projects.
3. **Also install the platformOS Agent Playbook?** — Claude Code only; see the next section.

Each tool keeps its MCP configuration in its own file, and `pos-cli ai init` writes the right one:

| Tool | This project | All your projects |
|---|---|---|
| Claude Code | `.mcp.json` | `~/.claude.json` |
| Cursor | `.cursor/mcp.json` | `~/.cursor/mcp.json` |
| VS Code | `.vscode/mcp.json` | printed for you to paste — its path depends on your platform and build |
| opencode | `opencode.json` | `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` |

Entries you have edited yourself are never overwritten: `pos-cli ai init` tells you what it would have written and leaves your version alone.

To skip the questions — in a setup script, for instance:

<pre class="command-line" data-user="user" data-host="host"><code class="language-bash">
pos-cli ai init --tool claude                  # local + documentation servers
pos-cli ai init --tool cursor --docs global    # documentation servers for all projects
pos-cli ai init --tool claude --no-docs        # local servers only
pos-cli ai init --tool claude --agent-playbook # also install the Agent Playbook
</code></pre>

## Install the platformOS Agent Playbook

The servers above let an agent look platformOS up, check code before it is written, and act on your instance. The **platformOS Agent Playbook** is the part of the Agent Kit that tells it which facts matter and in what order to work. It unpacks to a `.claude/` directory in your project and contains:

- **The order of work and the prohibitions**, loaded on every request — look the symbol up, validate the buffer, write, preview, deploy, fetch the page, read the data back. Writing a file and deploying it to find out whether it works is prohibited.
- **Per-directory rules**, loaded when the agent opens a file in that directory — pages, partials, layouts, commands, queries, GraphQL, schema and translations.
- **Topic files** it can consult on demand, covering the platformOS behaviour that fails silently and that no linter reports.
- **The platformOS coding agent**, a named agent for writing and changing platformOS code. The Playbook makes it the **default agent for the project**, so it handles platformOS work without you selecting it.
- **A diagnostics hook** that attaches the documented platformOS rule, and its fix, to a validation finding — so a terse error message arrives with the explanation beside it.

With the Playbook installed, Claude Code starts with that agent already selected:

<img loading="lazy" src="{{ 'images/get-started/ai-assisted-development/coding-agent.png' | asset_url }}" alt="Claude Code starting in a project with the Playbook installed, showing platformos-coding-agent as the active agent" width="889" height="597">

It is offered for Claude Code and no other tool, because that `.claude/` layout is Claude Code's own. The four MCP servers are not Claude-specific — Cursor, VS Code and opencode get all of them. Answer yes to the third question, or pass `--agent-playbook`; `--no-agent-playbook` declines without asking.

If you already have a `.claude/` directory it is never overwritten — it holds your own rules and settings. pos-cli says so and prints the download link instead, so you can unpack it somewhere harmless and merge in the parts you want:

<pre class="command-line" data-user="user" data-host="host"><code class="language-bash">
curl -fsSL https://documentation.platformos.com/assets/platformos-agent-playbook.zip -o playbook.zip
unzip -q playbook.zip -d playbook-tmp &amp;&amp; rm playbook.zip
</code></pre>

That leaves the Playbook at `playbook-tmp/.claude/`, next to your own `.claude/` rather than on top of it.

## Check that it works

Restart your AI tool so it picks up the new configuration, then ask it something only these servers can answer — "which platformOS environments are configured?" or "validate this Liquid snippet for me".

To see exactly what your tool was given, without starting it:

<pre class="command-line" data-user="user" data-host="host"><code class="language-bash">
pos-cli mcp-config
</code></pre>

That prints every tool the server exposes, every tool it is hiding and why, and the instructions sent to the client.

## What to expect

These tools act on a **real instance**. Three things are worth knowing before you hand an agent the keys:

- **A deploy is not a test.** `pos-cli ai init` registers a server whose default selection can deploy. Ask for a preview first — the agent has a dry-run tool that reports what a deploy would add, change and delete.
- **Validate before writing, not after.** `validate_code` checks a buffer. It only helps if the agent calls it with the content it is about to write.
- **Always name the environment.** With more than one environment in `.pos`, a call that could change an instance is refused until you say which — but with exactly one, it lands there with nothing asked.

For the full reference — every server, its tool profiles, and what the Playbook asserts — see [AI - Agent Kit](/developer-guide/ai/ai) in the Developer Guide.

